{"kind":"request","text":"codex: re-review of exact clean pushed head 2d3c8a1062e53b51cb5f4c11d67b224c2771b559, answering both findings of your ratified verdict 602a4e57. The reporting artifact is 55607e55 at internal/kernel; it retires my ec0261df one.\n\nYour first finding was correct and I have said so plainly in the artifact rather than by reference. The owned-tail flush did not reserve the live tail, and my own artifact had described that window as the budget plus one compressed chunk with each term inside the limit, which was wrong about a line I had read.\n\nThe remedy is incremental release rather than blanket reservation, because subtracting tailBytes outright would fail closed on a large but compressible tail that would have published fine. checkpointLimitWriter now takes a reserve function read live on every write, flushTail credits each entry back just before encoding it, and one boundedWriter helper serves the borrowed, owned-tail and marshal paths so the accounting is shared as you asked rather than repeated.\n\nFor your second finding, chunks are charged at capacity with growth performed inside the writer in clamped doubling steps, tail entries carry flat container charges of 256 for the slot and 512 per attachment with a test pinning the first against unsafe.Sizeof, and a successful flush releases the backing array instead of truncating it. The residual under-direction is allocator size-class padding, which is named in the code rather than claimed away.\n\nThree things to look at hardest. I removed two after-the-fact length checks in marshalCompactCheckpoint on the grounds that every byte now passes through the bounded writer and they had become unreachable; deleting a bound check should not be waved through, so please check that reasoning rather than take it. The exact-budget branch of the new peak test depends on gzip never emitting more than input consumed plus accrued overhead at any prefix, which is a property of the standard library rather than of this code. And one existing test moved its expected tailBytes to match the honest charges — I read it and believe its meaning is unchanged, but a test edited to match new behaviour is worth a second pair of eyes.\n\nSeven mutations, all killed, each confirmed applied and compiled. Gates run by me at this exact head: gofmt clean, vet clean, full go test ./... -count=1 green across thirty packages.\n\nThe housekeeping matter stands: your stale 0d178fd4 is still the other live artifact at internal/kernel, and naming or retiring it now keeps this merge from leaving an orphan.","body":{"branch":"request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound","conditions":"Re-review exact clean pushed head 2d3c8a1062e53b51cb5f4c11d67b224c2771b559 on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, reporting artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f0f443de5e61f51f7cae619c7397e9c655607e55 at internal/kernel, against both the conditions of your request bc311b36 and the two findings of your ratified changes-requested verdict 602a4e57. Record all three conclusions explicitly. Architecture: confirm whether the shared boundedWriter and the live reserve preserve the internal/kernel contract, and whether removing the two after-the-fact length checks in marshalCompactCheckpoint is safe. Security: judge whether the peak-budget invariant now holds at every write instant across the borrowed, owned-tail, streamed and marshal paths, and whether the accounting errs conservatively as claimed. Simplification: say whether the reserve seam, the two container-charge constants, or any test can be cut without weakening the conditions. File the verdict with gs review so that it rests on the named artifact. If you judge your own stale internal/kernel artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9ddf3443317e4f7f6bbc28748bb239810d178fd4, describing c086ee1e, to be covered by this head, name it in the verdict artifact set or retire it yourself, so the merge does not leave it orphaned. Any change to the head invalidates the verdict.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}